; An `(absent …)` whose sub-plan would compile to nothing.
;
; `?x` is not a premise: a premise compiler would return no steps for it, and
; an empty sub-plan does not mean "a query that finds nothing" — it matches
; VACUOUSLY, so the matcher's `holds` would be true and the guard would fail
; against every possible KB. The guard is monotone (no nested absent), so the
; candidate would be retired on its first judgement: a guard that never passes.
;
; The grammar refuses `?x` there at the parse, as a premise that is not a
; form, before any compiler sees it: a variable stands for a value, and
; `absent` reads a premise. Register case W24 is this shape's class, a rule
; shape that reads nothing, in silence; the guard this one may mean is
; written `(absent (p ?x))`.
(relation p Thing)

(rule vacuous-guard ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent ?x))
  :assert (p ?x))
